  "A2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently conventional over-jammed packings (CG) can be produced by quenching an equilibrium liquid heated from HG; and If the study strategy is to compare hyperuniform and conventional over-jammed parents and their decompressed marginally jammed descendants, then the algorithm should generate HG, produce CG as a control, and obtain JHG/JCG by decompression, therefore producing CG as described fits the comparative strategy."];
  "A3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently two marginally jammed states can be obtained by decompressing from HG and CG (JHG and JCG), with distinct jamming points; and If the study strategy is to compare hyperuniform and conventional over-jammed parents and their decompressed marginally jammed descendants, then the algorithm should generate HG, produce CG as a control, and obtain JHG/JCG by decompression, therefore obtaining JHG/JCG by decompression fits the comparative strategy."];
  "ARule" [label="(0,0,0) If the study strategy is to compare hyperuniform and conventional over-jammed parents and their decompressed marginally jammed descendants, then the algorithm should generate HG, produce CG as a control, and obtain JHG/JCG by decompression."];
  "BR3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given BRO’s Manna-class transition behavior and the suggested absorbing/dynamical interpretation of jamming, therefore an absorbing-critical mechanism is a coherent hypothesis motivating targeted comparative tests."];
  "BRORule" [label="(0,0,0) If a model (BRO) exhibits Manna-class absorbing transitions and reproduces α≈0.25 at criticality, then an absorbing-critical mechanism is a plausible explanation for α≈0.25 scaling seen near jamming."];
  "C2" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given the plausible absorbing-critical interpretation (Manna/BRO) for α≈0.25 and the value of hyperuniform disordered solids, therefore the comparative HG/CG and JHG/JCG study should focus on persistence of hyperuniformity in over-jammed states and associated anomalous (stability-related) properties."];
  "D1" [label="(0,0,0) If a system is hyperuniform (χ_v(q→0)→0), then it exhibits suppressed long-wavelength density fluctuations, which can underpin anomalous macroscopic properties."];
  "D2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently hyperuniform systems have χ_v(q→0)→0; and If a system is hyperuniform (χ_v(q→0)→0), then it exhibits suppressed long-wavelength density fluctuations, which can underpin anomalous macroscopic properties, therefore hyperuniformity implies suppressed long-wavelength density fluctuations relevant for properties."];
  "J3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given low-q deviations are established by both the observed deviation window and the possible α≈0.25 crossover, therefore testing low-q scaling across protocols is necessary."];
  "JRule" [label="(0,0,0) If marginally jammed states show low-q deviations and protocol-dependent differences, then comparing parent protocols and descendants is needed to determine universality vs protocol dependence of low-q scaling."];
  "K2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently hyperuniformity is often associated with critical phenomena, including jamming transitions and absorbing transitions; and If hyperuniform systems are classified by low-q scaling and occur across critical contexts (e.g., jamming/absorbing), then detailed low-q characterization and mechanism testing are scientifically motivated, therefore low-q characterization/mechanism tests are scientifically motivated."];
  "KRule" [label="(0,0,0) If hyperuniform systems are classified by low-q scaling and occur across critical contexts (e.g., jamming/absorbing), then detailed low-q characterization and mechanism testing are scientifically motivated."];
  "MRJ2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently the expected linear scaling for MRJ has only been confirmed for q/2π > 0.1 (not deep into the low-q regime); and If MRJ is conjectured to be class II (linear χ_v(q) at small q) but confirmation is only at relatively large q, then improved methods/samples are needed to test true low-q behavior, therefore improved methods/samples are needed to test true low-q behavior for MRJ-related questions."];
  "MRJRule" [label="(0,0,0) If MRJ is conjectured to be class II (linear χ_v(q) at small q) but confirmation is only at relatively large q, then improved methods/samples are needed to test true low-q behavior."];
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, a defining feature of hyperuniform systems is that the density spectrum χ_v(q) vanishes as q→0, unlike ordinary liquids/solids."];
  "N10" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, the low-q deviation either saturates to a constant or crosses over to a new power law with exponent α≈0.25."];
  "N11" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, the α≈0.25 low-q scaling resembles the critical state of Manna-class absorbing phase transitions."];
  "N12" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, the BRO model was introduced and it exhibits active–absorbing transitions in the Manna universality class."];
  "N13" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, at its critical maximum-density point, BRO reproduces RCP density (~0.64) and RCP-like hyperuniform scaling with α≈0.25."];
  "N14" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, these observations suggest that the RCP/jamming transition can be interpreted as an absorbing/dynamical phase transition."];
  "N15" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, marginally jammed-state characteristics depend on preparation protocol; the resulting jamming points form a jamming line."];
  "N16" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, for denser marginally jammed states made by SMC, χ_v(q) deviates more from linearity and becomes less hyperuniform."];
  "N17" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, it is unclear whether hyperuniformity can persist in over-jammed states and what anomalous properties it would impart."];
  "N18" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, the study proposes creating hyperuniform over-jammed packings (hyperuniform glasses, HG) via an iterative algorithm combining particle resizing and energy minimisation at φ well above φ_J."];
  "N19" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, for comparison, conventional over-jammed packings (CG) can be produced by quenching an equilibrium liquid heated from HG."];
  "N2" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, hyperuniform states are classified by χ_v(q→0) ~ q^α: class I (α>1), class II (α=1), class III (0<α<1)."];
  "N20" [label="(28,0,0) Currently, two marginally jammed states can be obtained by decompressing from HG and CG (JHG and JCG), with distinct jamming points (0.666 vs 0.648)."];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) If (i) over-jammed hyperuniformity is unclear and protocol dependence complicates conclusions, then constructing controlled over-jammed hyperuniform and conventional glasses and comparing their (de)compressed marginally jammed descendants is a suitable strategy to test persistence, protocol dependence, and anomalous properties."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently over-jammed hyperuniformity and its properties are unclear and protocol effects exist; and If (i) over-jammed hyperuniformity is unclear and protocol dependence complicates conclusions, then constructing controlled over-jammed hyperuniform and conventional glasses and comparing their (de)compressed marginally jammed descendants is a suitable strategy to test persistence, protocol dependence, and anomalous properties, therefore we should construct HG/CG and compare JHG/JCG to address them."];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) If a target is to study hyperuniformity persistence and anomalous properties in over-jammed states, then one must (a) generate an over-jammed state intended to be hyperuniform and (b) generate a matched conventional over-jammed control for comparison."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently the research target is to test hyperuniformity persistence/anomalous properties in over-jammed states; and If a target is to study hyperuniformity persistence and anomalous properties in over-jammed states, then one must (a) generate an over-jammed state intended to be hyperuniform and (b) generate a matched conventional over-jammed control for comparison, therefore the proposal should include constructing HG and CG."];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) If preparation protocols affect marginally jammed properties, then decompressing from two different over-jammed parent states provides a direct protocol/parent-history test at jamming."];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently marginally jammed properties depend on protocol (jamming line); and If preparation protocols affect marginally jammed properties, then decompressing from two different over-jammed parent states provides a direct protocol/parent-history test at jamming, therefore we should create JHG and JCG by decompression from HG and CG."];
  "N30" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given Phenomenon: jamming shows α≈0.25 low-q scaling resembling Manna criticality; and Knowledge: BRO/Manna criticality yields α≈0.25 at criticality, therefore a plausible explanatory route is that jamming behavior is tied to an absorbing critical mechanism, motivating targeted comparative tests using HG/CG and JHG/JCG."];
  "N31" [label="(0,0,0) If disordered hyperuniform materials have unique properties, then creating a stable over-jammed disordered hyperuniform solid (a hyperuniform glass) is scientifically valuable, especially to assess anomalous properties relative to conventional glasses and marginally jammed states."];
  "N32" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently disordered hyperuniformity implies unusual/exceptional properties; and If disordered hyperuniform materials have unique properties, then creating a stable over-jammed disordered hyperuniform solid (a hyperuniform glass) is scientifically valuable, especially to assess anomalous properties relative to conventional glasses and marginally jammed states, therefore the research should create HG and compare to CG (and their jamming descendants)."];
  "N4" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, this combination can yield exceptional electronic/transport/optical properties (e.g., isotropic photonic band gaps)."];
  "N5" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, hyperuniformity is often associated with critical phenomena, including jamming transitions and absorbing transitions."];
  "N6" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, MRJ packings were conjectured to be class II hyperuniform, i.e., χ_v(q) shows linear scaling at small q."];
  "N8" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, the expected linear scaling for MRJ has only been confirmed for q/2π > 0.1 (not deep into the low-q regime)."];
  "N9" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, for marginally jammed states, χ_v(q) is linear at intermediate q (0.08<q/2π<0.4) but deviates at lower q (q/2π<0.08)."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate an iterative particle-resizing/energy-minimisation approach to create over-jammed hyperuniform glasses, construct matched conventional-glass controls, and compare their decompressed marginally jammed states to test the persistence of density hyperuniformity, protocol-dependent low-q scaling, absorbing-critical mechanisms, and anomalous stability-related properties.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Convergent motivations from hyperuniformity theory, MRJ low-q limitations, protocol-dependent jamming behavior, BRO/Manna critical analogies, and HG/CG/JHG/JCG construction support a comparative study of over-jammed hyperuniform glasses and their marginally jammed descendants."];
  "P1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Currently for denser marginally jammed states made by SMC, χ_v(q) deviates more from linearity and becomes less hyperuniform; and If denser marginally jammed states produced by a specific protocol show reduced hyperuniformity, then protocol/parent-history effects materially impact χ_v(q) and must be explicitly tested, therefore protocol effects on χ_v(q) must be explicitly tested."];
  "PRule" [label="(0,0,0) If denser marginally jammed states produced by a specific protocol show reduced hyperuniformity, then protocol/parent-history effects materially impact χ_v(q) and must be explicitly tested."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The classification of hyperuniform states by low-q scaling, together with their association with critical phenomena such as jamming and absorbing transitions, motivates detailed low-q characterization and mechanism testing."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The conjecture that MRJ packings are class-II hyperuniform, together with the fact that the expected linear scaling has only been confirmed outside the deep low-q regime, motivates improved methods or samples for testing true low-q behavior."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Observed low-q deviations in marginally jammed states, combined with known protocol dependence of marginally jammed characteristics, motivate comparing preparation histories to assess whether low-q scaling is universal or protocol dependent."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The possible low-q saturation or α≈0.25 crossover, combined with known protocol dependence of marginally jammed states, motivates protocol-aware comparisons of low-q scaling near jamming."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Because BRO exhibits Manna-class absorbing transitions and reproduces α≈0.25 scaling at its critical maximum-density point, an absorbing-critical mechanism is a plausible hypothesis for α≈0.25 scaling seen near jamming."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) BRO’s reproduction of RCP-like α≈0.25 hyperuniform scaling and the suggested absorbing/dynamical interpretation of the RCP/jamming transition jointly support treating an absorbing-critical mechanism as a plausible explanatory route to test."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Concrete reduced hyperuniformity in denser SMC marginally jammed states, together with the broader jamming-line protocol dependence of marginally jammed characteristics, supports explicitly testing protocol or parent-history effects on χ_v(q)."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) The proposed workflow contains the elements needed for the comparative strategy: generating HG by the iterative particle-resizing/energy-minimisation algorithm, producing CG as a conventional control, and obtaining JHG and JCG by decompression."];
  "R34" [label="(0,0,0) The need to test low-q scaling across protocols and the coherent absorbing-critical hypothesis jointly motivate targeted comparative tests of jamming-related hyperuniform scaling."];
  "R35" [label="(0,0,0) Both the MRJ class-II conjecture and the limited confirmation of linear scaling outside the deepest low-q regime support the need for methodological improvements or alternative algorithms for low-q tests."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Because disordered hyperuniform materials can have exceptional properties, creating a stable over-jammed disordered hyperuniform solid is scientifically valuable for exploring anomalous properties."];
